# Goal — precision-filter candidate principle clusters, then stop
You are the PRECISION FILTER of a recall-then-filter merge. A deterministic embedding-recall step
over-grouped operational principles distilled from 9 software-architecture books into candidate
clusters — embedding similarity is NOT equivalence, so some clusters fuse distinct principles. Decide,
per cluster, whether its members are truly the same principle. AUTHORING ONLY: write just the one
decisions file below; do not modify anything else, do not commit.

# Input
Read `{{WORK}}/clusters.json` — a JSON list of clusters, each `{cluster_id, members:[{pid, source_id, statement}]}`.

# Per cluster choose exactly ONE action
- **confirm** — the members ARE the same operational principle, re-stated across books. Provide a single
  `canonical` statement that captures the shared principle faithfully (NEVER stronger/broader than the
  members; keep any hedges/conditions they share).
- **split** — the members are DISTINCT principles that merely sound alike. Provide `subgroups`: a list of
  pid-lists; each subgroup becomes one principle (a singleton subgroup stands alone). EVERY member pid
  must appear in exactly one subgroup.
- **conflict** — the members DISAGREE (opposing guidance), not duplicate. Keep them separate; downstream
  reconcile handles it.

Judge by OPERATIONAL meaning, not surface wording: confirm only when a reviewer would apply the members
interchangeably. **When unsure between confirm and split, SPLIT** — preserving a distinct principle is
safer than fusing two. Large clusters (e.g. the 13-member one) are almost certainly multiple principles
→ split into coherent subgroups.

# Output — write `{{WORK}}/decisions.json` and NOTHING else there: a JSON list, one object per cluster:
  {"cluster_id":"K01","action":"confirm","canonical":"<merged statement>"}
  {"cluster_id":"K02","action":"split","subgroups":[["G0007"],["G0042","G0101"]]}
  {"cluster_id":"K03","action":"conflict"}
Cover EVERY cluster_id present in clusters.json. The file must be valid JSON (no prose, no trailing commas).

